course: dictionary
lesson: 5656
title: Lit: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: «Lit» is the past form of «light». Today you light a candle; yesterday you «lit» one. «Lit» can also describe a place or thing that has light falling on it, as in «the room was lit by lamps».
explain: «Lit up» means shining brightly, or a face showing happiness. «Her face lit up» tells you she suddenly looked happy. Use the words around lit to choose its meaning: a candle, a fire or a room means light, while a face or a smile means happiness.
explain: Lit things need care. Lit candles, lit matches and a lit fire must never be left alone, and you should not light them without an adult. In slang, lit means exciting, but that is informal, so do not use it in school writing.
fact: «Lit» is the past form of «light»: light, lights, lit, lighting.
fact: «Lit» means having light or burning, as in «lit candles».
fact: «Lit up» means shining brightly, or showing happiness: «her face lit up».
fact: «The far side is fully lit» at new moon, so the far side of the Moon is not always dark.
fact: Things are lit when light falls on them; dark places are not lit.
exercise: «The room was lit by lamps.» What does lit mean here? | Was painted bright blue | *Was lit by lamps and so had light | Had light from lamps | Was very noisy
exercise: «Her face lit up when she opened the present.» What does lit up mean? | *Shone with happiness | Became dirty | Fell asleep | Turned off
exercise: «He lit a candle on the cake.» Find and copy the past form of light in this sentence. | candle | *lit | lighting | lights
test: «The lit candles must never be left alone.» What does lit mean here? | Made of wax | Blown out and cold | Hidden under a cloth | *Burning and giving light
test: Ella says lit means exciting, so she wants to use it in her school story. Why should she not? | *It is informal slang, not for school writing | It is the same as light, so it is wrong | It means dark and cold | It is a name for a town
exam: Which sentence uses lit correctly as the past form of light? | Yesterday Mum lighting the stove. | Yesterday Mum lights the stove. | *Yesterday Mum lit the stove. | Yesterday Mum lits the stove.
